# FastAPI testing

Narrows `python`'s `pytest` conventions to FastAPI's own test client and
dependency-override patterns. Applies only to FastAPI project files; see
`skills/fastapi-testing/SKILL.md` for the full test-authoring workflow this
rule file backs.

## Test client

- Test path operations through `fastapi.testclient.TestClient` (sync tests)
  or `httpx.AsyncClient` with `ASGITransport` (async tests) against the
  real `app` instance, rather than calling the path operation function
  directly — calling the function directly skips FastAPI's own request
  parsing, dependency resolution, and response-model filtering, which is
  exactly what most bugs in a path operation live in.
- Build the `TestClient`/`AsyncClient` from a fixture shared across a test
  module (or `conftest.py` once a second module needs it), not
  re-instantiated per test, unless a test needs a differently configured
  app instance.

## Dependency overrides

- Override a dependency for a test with `app.dependency_overrides[real_dep]
  = fake_dep`, not by monkeypatching the dependency function's module
  attribute — `dependency_overrides` is FastAPI's own supported mechanism
  and is what keeps the override scoped to the app instance under test.
- Clear `app.dependency_overrides` after each test that sets one (a fixture
  `yield`ing the override and clearing it in teardown, or `.clear()` in the
  test itself) — an override left in place leaks into unrelated tests run
  afterward in the same process.
- Use a dependency override for the current-user/auth dependency to test
  both an authenticated and an unauthenticated path without standing up
  real credential handling in every test.

## Request/response assertions

- Assert on the parsed response body (`response.json()`) and
  `response.status_code`, not just that the call didn't raise — a path
  operation that returns the wrong status code or a malformed body with no
  exception is a real bug a bare "it didn't crash" assertion misses.
- For a validation-error case, assert the `422` status FastAPI returns for
  a Pydantic validation failure, not a generic exception — a request that
  fails Pydantic validation never reaches the path operation body at all.
- When `response_model` is declared, assert that a field the model
  excludes (e.g. `hashed_password`) is actually absent from the response
  body for at least one test — this is the behavior `response_model`
  exists to guarantee, and a typo in the model's field list would
  otherwise ship unnoticed.

## Database and external dependencies

- Use a real test database (a throwaway SQLite file, a test-scoped
  Postgres schema, or the project's own configured test-DB fixture) behind
  a `get_db`-style dependency override, not a hand-rolled mock of the ORM
  session, for anything beyond the simplest unit test — an ORM session
  mock only checks that the code called the mock the way the mock expects.
- Mock genuinely external services (a third-party HTTP API, an email
  provider) at the client boundary (`httpx`/`requests` mock, or the
  project's configured HTTP mocking library), matching `python`'s own
  testing rule: mock external dependencies, not internal collaborators.

## Async tests

- Mark an async test `pytest.mark.asyncio` (or the project's configured
  async plugin/mode) exactly as `python`'s testing rule requires; this
  applies the same way to a test that awaits `AsyncClient` calls against a
  FastAPI app.

## Coverage expectations

- Every new or touched path operation gets at least one test for its
  success path, its validation-failure path (a `422` for bad input when
  the body is a Pydantic model), and, when it requires auth, both an
  authenticated and an unauthenticated case.
- A dependency with nontrivial logic (parses a header, decodes a token,
  applies a business rule) gets its own test independent of any path
  operation that happens to use it, in addition to the path operation's
  own integration-level test.
